WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.980
Concorde, the name recalls a distant era

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.040
when air travel was more like this-

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.840
Welcome aboard the spacious cabin.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.810
Than this.
Oh my .

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.493
Someone help, this guy's crazy!

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.550
Almost two decades

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.820
after the Concorde's last
supersonic leap across the ocean,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.850
Concorde nostalgia suddenly
seems in full swing.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.436
All chairs will have tray
tables, not just first class.

00:00:27.436 --> 00:00:30.240
I feel like I'm flying on the Concorde.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.200
You can leave any time.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.770
Meanwhile, a new generation
of companies is promising

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.460
to bring supersonic jets
back to an airport near you

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.650
by as soon as the end of this decade,

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.070
but can any of these
companies successfully pick up

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.983
where the failed Concorde left off?

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.740
So the Concorde operated
in the North Atlantic.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.900
It was primarily Paris and London

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.103
to New York and Washington.

00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:06.442
And we're now supersonic.

00:01:06.442 --> 00:01:09.250
That's 11 minutes after takeoff.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.930
It's always exciting flying supersonic,

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.840
and it's always exciting
to get to New York

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.600
before you've left.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.560
There was a large measure
of style and panache,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.930
and it wasn't the world's most
comfortable cabin or seat,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.730
compared to what you would
get on a private jet today,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.120
but it was also the bragging rights

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.770
of how fast you could do
a day trip, if you wanted.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.050
Concorde has crossed the
Atlantic in 3.5 hours.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.403
First, best, and fastest.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.230
It just never became a
product for the masses,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.200
and part of that was just the fuel cost.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.570
These engines were engineering marvels,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.280
but at the same time, just
completely fuel inefficient,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.893
in terms of what they had to do.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.780
After nearly three decades in service,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.730
the Concorde was retired in
2003, due to rising costs,

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.510
weakening demand for
air travel after 9/11,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.190
and a dampened safety reputation,

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.980
following a fatal crash in 2000.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.850
Once at the bleeding edge
of aviation technology,

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.590
the legendary plane for the
elite had become a relic,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.023
destined for museums and exhibitions.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:12.890
Today, whether you're paying

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.880
to ride back here or up here,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.030
you'll arrive at about the same time,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.080
and that's because aircraft manufacturers

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.060
and airlines have spent the
past half-century focusing

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.530
on things like efficiency,
safety, and cabin comforts,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.903
rather than raw speed.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.770
But after almost two decades
since Concorde's demise,

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.633
speed may be making a comeback.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.260
There is venture capital
money and investors

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.770
that are willing to bet
that this could work.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.320
Secondly, there's
technology available today

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.150
that was never, ever
contemplated in the 1960s.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.530
You can model so many
things with computers,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.140
and the new materials, carbon composites,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.680
all those things are coming together

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.307
so that you have some
technically minded people saying,

00:02:57.307 --> 00:02:58.977
"Why shouldn't this happen?"

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.610
When Concorde shut down
about 20 years ago,

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.810
there was really no plan to
pick up where it left off,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.300
and so that's what we're doing at Boom.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.250
We're building high-speed travel

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.950
that can be available to
more people than ever before.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.010
Boom is just one of a
number of young companies

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.980
that have snatched up the
brightest aerospace engineers

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.880
for the task of designing a new generation

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.970
of supersonic aircraft.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.990
Every airplane looks good on paper,

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.780
but when you go from a paper
design to a physical airplane

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.220
that you're gonna put a human
life in and then go fly,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.880
you have to solve thousands
of problems along the way,

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.870
but it's not inventing new science

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.330
and it's not inventing new technology.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:41.280
Boom has been developing

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.610
an experimental supersonic
jet called the XB-1,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.410
which, if successful, will be-

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.660
The first independently
developed supersonic jet ever.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:50.810
With a name that hearkens back

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.270
to the original supersonic jet, the X-1,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.810
the XB-1 will be used to test
and refine the engineering

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.750
that will ultimately lead to a
100-seat jet called overture.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.170
It's more like a Concorde
2.0, and if all goes well-

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.620
We'll be carrying our first passengers

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.330
by the end of the decade.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.560
But building a commercial plane

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.510
that can break the sound barrier
is old news at this point.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.420
I think Google could probably go build

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.170
a supersonic business jet

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:19.520
in pretty short order
if they wanted to, one,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.540
not to sell it, but just to do it.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.580
It's just gotta be an economical product.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.530
You can't just do it as a vanity thing.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.020
The first commercial
supersonic experiments

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:33.433
in the 1960s were essentially
that, vanity projects.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.400
The jointly built British and French jet,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.260
literally a concord between
nations, and rival prototypes

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.640
from the Soviets and the
Americans were developed

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.090
during the same Cold War technology race

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:46.640
that landed humans on the moon.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.220
National prestige outweighed
concerns about profitability,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.000
at least for a little while.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.180
What about the potential
market for Concorde?

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.900
What's the lowest they could be sold

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.230
and, perhaps, the highest?

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.510
If we sold 50 or less,

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:03.630
I should be a very disappointed man.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.250
Disappointed indeed, though it started out

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.140
with over 100 pre-orders.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.530
The jet's problematic
economics quickly brought

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.710
that number down to just 14,

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.260
delivered to the national airlines

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.580
of the countries that built it.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.780
But the next generation of
supersonics are being designed

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.180
mostly by private companies,

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.960
where market viability isn't

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.800
merely an afterthought, it's paramount.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.040
Just as Concorde retired in '03,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.520
there's been a 2.5-fold increase
in international travel,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.930
and that creates a lot more
markets for supersonic.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.840
And when you put together
all the new technology,

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.290
aerodynamics, materials, propulsion,

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:39.720
you can now build an aircraft

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.430
that's significantly more
efficient than Concorde,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.800
which means it can be available

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.130
to tens of millions of people

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.830
at fares like what you'd
pay in business class today,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:50.270
instead of the the Forex first class

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.080
that had to be charged on Concorde.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.530
So far, Boom's bold
aspirations have already caught

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.600
the attention of a handful of airlines

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:58.960
that have placed small pre-orders

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.160
for the company's Overture jet,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.460
but whether these early votes
of confidence will translate

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.223
into success depends
on the final economics.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.700
Another eager startup
taking a different approach

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.810
to the new supersonic
race is Spike Aerospace,

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.050
which is developing the S-512,

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.410
an 18-passenger aircraft targeted

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:23.010
at the nearly $30 billion
private business jet market.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.540
Like Boom, though, one of
Spike's long-term goals is

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.530
to eventually expand supersonic travel

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.170
beyond the realm of elite luxury.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.443
We'd looked back to the 90s,

00:06:31.443 --> 00:06:33.593
when they first had flat-screen TVs.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.530
They were $25,000 a screen.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.150
Nobody could afford it.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:39.430
Joe Public couldn't afford it.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.210
It's only for the super rich,

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.870
but now, flat-screen TVs are all $200,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.140
and the same thing's gonna
happen to supersonic flight.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.690
Yes, initially, it probably
will be for business travelers

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.550
and the rich folks.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.960
There is a demand, they have a need,

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.620
but can afford to fly it.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.220
As we recover cost

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.620
and research and development
and technologies,

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.370
we'll be able to offer that
same supersonic flight service

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:02.040
to the general public.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.040
I don't know that it'll be
as cheap as economy flights,

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.810
but it'll be affordable,
or more affordable

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.110
than it will be initially.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.620
Before these next-generation
supersonics can make it

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.950
to market, though, there are
other dark legacies of Concorde

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.213
that could divert a supersonic comeback.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.283
The first is simply too loud to ignore.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.010
What did you think about it?
Oh, it makes you jump.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:24.966
It made you jump, did it?
Yes,

00:07:24.966 --> 00:07:26.610
oh, goodness.
You frightened of it at all?

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.229
No, not exactly.
What did you think

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:31.235
of it, sir?
Rather slow one, I thought.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.500
The deafening takeoff noise
and infamous sonic booms

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.520
of supersonic aircraft
crossing the sound barrier were

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.720
so disruptive that it led
to widespread backlash

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.030
and protests in the 1960s,
eventually forcing governments

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.473
to ban all supersonic flight over land,

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.480
but there's a potential
solution taking shape

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.230
that could address both the physics

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.893
and the regulation
dilemmas of the sonic boom.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.100
X-59 is, really, a culmination
of decades of work by NASA

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:03.423
to try and solve the sonic boom problem,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:07.710
essentially to find a
way to reduce sonic booms

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.390
such that it's not a boom anymore,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:14.870
but a sound that might be
acceptable if heard every day.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.890
To accomplish that
goal, NASA has partnered

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.650
with Lockheed Martin's
famous Skunk Works division

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.740
to develop an advanced airframe design

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.370
that can actually minimize the impact

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.220
of the sonic shockwave on the ground.

00:08:26.642 --> 00:08:28.110
But the heart of the project is

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.030
about gauging public reaction
in order to pave the way

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:34.560
for new supersonic federal
and international guidelines.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.830
Our plan is to do,
essentially, five to six tests

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:42.770
in various locations in the United States

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:43.956
to hopefully get on the order

00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:47.040
of thousands of survey responses.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.290
Given a sound of a certain level,

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.470
this percentage of the
population is annoyed.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.700
So it's all about providing data

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.430
that can help the FAA and
the ICAO establish the limits

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.780
that go along with this
certification standard.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.110
Reducing or eliminating the nuisance

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.620
of sonic booms could open
up countless new routes,

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.210
helping to make an economy of
scale for supersonic travel

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.060
that was never possible for Concorde.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.960
But there's another drawback
of supersonic flight

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.430
that has taken on a new importance

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:21.753
over the past few decades,
fuel consumption and emissions.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.930
In order to propel its 100-seat cabin

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:28.340
to speeds of nearly 1,400
miles per hour, Concorde burned

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.920
multiple times the amount
of fuel per passenger,

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.800
versus a typical transoceanic jet.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.870
Given the fluctuating costs of
fuel and the growing concern

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.250
over the environmental
impacts of air travel,

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.040
it's hard to imagine a modern
carrier purchasing planes

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.630
as thirsty and messy as
the Concorde once was.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:47.950
But recent technological strides may give

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.440
the supersonic startups a chance

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.340
to rectify this negative legacy, as well.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:53.410
Speed is not enough.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.310
We need to do it in an
environmentally responsible way.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.600
And so the question really for us was

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.970
can we engineer these liquid fuels,

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.140
instead of just relying
on fossil-based fuels

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.650
that are obviously bad
for our environment?

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:07.483
Aerion has partnered

00:10:07.483 --> 00:10:10.080
with Canada-based Carbon Engineering,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.360
whose Direct Air Capture technology pulls

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.530
CO2 right out of the air,
which can then be processed

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.480
into commercial products like jet fuel.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.350
It's still a bit
theoretical at this point,

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.740
but it would mean, essentially,

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:24.190
carbon-neutral high-speed flight.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:26.320
So think about the only CO2

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.230
that the AS2, Aerion's airplane,

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.380
puts into the atmosphere
was first CO2 we took

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.700
out of the air to make the fuel.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.540
So not only do we go really, really fast,

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.193
but we have no footprint
on the environment.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.420
While all of these
supersonic startups are eager

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.520
for us to break the sound barrier again,

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.680
it's hard not to be at
least a little bit skeptical

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:48.690
of the projections,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.693
given how notoriously difficult
aircraft development can be.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.160
For instance, Boeing's
wide-body 787 Dreamliner

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.710
reportedly ran billions
of dollars over budget

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.720
and arrived to market three
years later than expected,

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.100
and that's for a jet based largely

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.050
on proven subsonic technology

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.393
and well-proven subsonic economics.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.860
There's also the unknown
long-term impact on demand

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:12.690
in a post-pandemic world,

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.240
in which business or VIP travel
may be replaced more often

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:18.680
by a virtual meeting instead.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.520
Then again, supersonic might
actually offer extra appeal

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.200
for travelers trying to
cut down on the time spent

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.920
in an enclosed cabin with
a bunch of other people.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.920
The customer wants 100 things.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.280
I think that's where the
rubber hits the road,

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.750
or whatever the cliche is.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:33.600
That's where you're gonna see.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.210
It's not can you build this plan,

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:38.600
but can you do it within
all these parameters

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.350
that the market is setting for you?

00:11:42.265 --> 00:11:44.400
Another way to look at
the new supersonic race is

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.810
that it's part of a
broader vision for a future

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.820
with simply more options
for long-distance travel,

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:53.560
whether by supersonic, hypersonic,
or an entirely new mode,

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.790
like suborbital flights,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.110
that hop between points on the globe.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.490
This latest round of startups
are simply pushing us

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.540
toward that frontier.

00:12:01.823 --> 00:12:03.800
And that might sound a
little pie-in-the-sky,

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.910
but when you're trying
to usher in a new age

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.300
of super-fast travel, it
helps to be optimistic.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.640
We're gonna go across the Atlantic

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.020
and come home the same day.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:13.610
We're gonna have more meetings in person.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.500
It's gonna change who you
can fall in love with.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:16.333
I want a world

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:19.330
where no relationship is ever
considered long-distance.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:20.680
That's not science fiction.

